            },
            {
                "text": "official VA rate table",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/compensation-rates/veteran-rates/"
            },
            {
                "text": "."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "HOW VA RATINGS WORK"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "The VA rates each service-connected condition from 0% to 100% in 10% increments using the Schedule for Rating Disabilities ("
            },
            {
                "text": "38 CFR Part 4",
                "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-4"
            },
            {
                "text": "). A 0% rating means the condition is service-connected but is not currently producing a compensable level of impairment."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Each diagnostic code has its own criteria. The VA assigns the rating based on the evidence in the claims file — typically service treatment records, private and VA medical records, lay statements, and the Compensation & Pension (C&P) exam."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "COMBINED RATINGS"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "When a veteran has multiple conditions, the VA combines them using the table at "
            },
            {
                "text": "38 CFR §4.25",
                "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-4#4.25"
            },
            {
                "text": " — not simple addition. Each rating reduces the \"remaining efficiency\" of a hypothetical healthy person, and the result is rounded to the nearest 10%."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "callout",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Quick example.",
                "bold": true
            },
            {
                "text": " A 50% rating combined with another 50% rating yields 75% under §4.25 (50% takes half of the remaining 50%), which rounds to 80%. Two 50% conditions do not combine to 100%."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "A "
            },
            {
                "text": "bilateral factor",
                "bold": true
            },
            {
                "text": " adds 10% to the combined value of paired-extremity conditions (e.g., both knees, both hands) before final rounding ("
            },
            {
                "text": "38 CFR §4.26",
                "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-4#4.26"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "EVIDENCE THE VA CONSIDERS"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "The VA generally considers service treatment records, post-service medical records, VA medical records, Disability Benefits Questionnaires (DBQs), lay statements (sometimes called \"buddy statements\"), and the C&P exam report ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — Evidence needed",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/how-to-file-claim/evidence-needed/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "You can submit private medical records and a private-provider DBQ in addition to whatever the VA gathers. The DBQ library is published at "
            },
            {
                "text": "Benefits.va.gov — DBQs",
                "url": "https://www.benefits.va.gov/COMPENSATION/dbq_disabilityexams.asp"
            },
            {
                "text": "."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "HOW TO FILE A CLAIM"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Initial disability claims are filed on "
            },
            {
                "text": "VA Form 21-526EZ",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/find-forms/about-form-21-526ez/"
            },
            {
                "text": ". You can file online, by mail, in person, or with the help of an accredited Veterans Service Organization ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — How to file",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/how-to-file-claim/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Filing with an accredited VSO is free to the veteran. VSOs are accredited under VA Office of General Counsel rules and can prepare, present, and prosecute the claim on the veteran's behalf ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA accreditation search",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/ogc/apps/accreditation/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "INTENT TO FILE & EFFECTIVE DATES"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Submitting an "
            },
            {
                "text": "Intent to File (VA Form 21-0966)",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/find-forms/about-form-21-0966/"
            },
            {
                "text": " can preserve a potential effective date for up to one year while you gather evidence and complete the formal claim. If the claim is later granted, benefits may be paid back to the Intent to File date rather than the date the formal claim was submitted ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — When to file",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/how-to-file-claim/when-to-file/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "CLAIM TYPES"
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Initial claim"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Used when you have never been rated for a condition, or you are claiming a new condition. Filed on VA Form 21-526EZ ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/how-to-file-claim/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Supplemental claim"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Used after a decision when you have "
            },
            {
                "text": "new and relevant evidence",
                "bold": true
            },
            {
                "text": " the VA has not previously considered ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — Supplemental Claim",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/decision-reviews/supplemental-claim/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Higher-Level Review"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "A senior reviewer takes a fresh look at the existing record. No new evidence is allowed ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — Higher-Level Review",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/decision-reviews/higher-level-review/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "text": "Board appeal"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Reviewed by a Veterans Law Judge at the Board of Veterans' Appeals. The Board offers three dockets (direct, evidence, hearing) ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — Board Appeal",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/decision-reviews/board-appeal/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "C&P EXAMS"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "A Compensation & Pension exam may be scheduled to evaluate the claimed condition. The exam is conducted by VA clinicians or by contractors working with VA ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — VA claim exam",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/va-claim-exam/"
            },
            {
                "text": "). The examiner typically completes a DBQ and writes findings the rater will use."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "VA's published guidance is to attend the exam, bring records relevant to the condition, and describe symptoms accurately, including on flare-up days. Missing the exam without rescheduling can lead to a denial."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "DEPENDENTS, SMC, AID & ATTENDANCE, HOUSEBOUND"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Added dependent pay begins at the 30% rating tier. Amounts for a spouse, child, or dependent parent are published on the same VA rate page as the base rates ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — Veteran rates",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/compensation-rates/veteran-rates/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Special Monthly Compensation (SMC)",
                "bold": true
            },
            {
                "text": " is additional compensation for specific, severe situations such as loss of use of a limb, blindness, or the need for aid and attendance. SMC categories and rates are published at "
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — SMC rates",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/compensation-rates/special-monthly-compensation-rates/"
            },
            {
                "text": " and governed by "
            },
            {
                "text": "38 CFR §3.350",
                "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-3/subpart-A/section-3.350"
            },
            {
                "text": "."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Aid and Attendance",
                "bold": true
            },
            {
                "text": " and "
            },
            {
                "text": "Housebound",
                "bold": true
            },
            {
                "text": " benefits are additional payments for veterans who need help with daily activities or are substantially confined to home ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — Aid & Attendance / Housebound",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/pension/aid-attendance-housebound/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "TDIU (TOTAL DISABILITY INDIVIDUAL UNEMPLOYABILITY)"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "TDIU allows a veteran to be paid at the 100% compensation rate without a 100% schedular rating when service-connected conditions prevent substantially gainful employment ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — TDIU",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/eligibility/special-claims/unemployability/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "The schedular thresholds under "
            },
            {
                "text": "38 CFR §4.16",
                "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-4/subpart-A/section-4.16"
            },
            {
                "text": " are generally a single condition rated at 60% or higher, or a combined rating of 70% with at least one condition at 40%. The VA may also consider extraschedular TDIU outside these thresholds."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "PACT ACT (TOXIC EXPOSURE)"
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "The PACT Act expanded VA health care and benefits for veterans exposed to toxic substances during service, including burn pits, Agent Orange, and radiation. It added presumptive conditions and broadened eligibility for affected eras ("
            },
            {
                "text": "VA.gov — PACT Act",
                "url": "https://www.va.gov/resources/the-pact-act-and-your-va-benefits/"
            },
            {
                "text": ")."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "If you served in a covered location and time period and have a covered condition, the VA presumes service connection. You can still file even if your condition is not on the presumptive list — direct service connection remains available."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 2,
        "text": "COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID"
    },
    {
        "type": "list",
        "ordered": false,
        "items": [
            {
                "spans": [
                    {
                        "text": "Filing only the condition you remember and not the related conditions in your record. The VA decides what you claim — not what you could have claimed ("
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "VA.gov",
                        "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/how-to-file-claim/"
                    },
                    {
                        "text": ")."
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "spans": [
                    {
                        "text": "Missing the C&P exam without rescheduling, which can lead to a denial ("
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "VA.gov — C&P exams",
                        "url": "https://www.va.gov/disability/va-claim-exam/"
                    },
