        "text": "FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS"
    },
    {
        "type": "faq_item",
        "question": "Is VA disability permanent?",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Not automatically. The VA can propose a reduction if it finds the condition has improved. Continuous ratings receive additional protection at 5, 10, and 20 years under 38 CFR §3.951 and §3.957. A \"Permanent and Total\" designation removes scheduled re-exams. Confirm your status on VA.gov."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "faq_item",
        "question": "Is VA disability taxable?",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "No. VA disability compensation is exempt from federal income tax per the IRS. Most states also exempt it, but state treatment varies — check your state tax authority."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "faq_item",
        "question": "Can I work and collect VA disability?",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "A veteran rated at 100% schedular may continue to work. Total Disability based on Individual Unemployability (TDIU) has earnings limits tied to substantially gainful employment. See VA.gov for the current rules."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "faq_item",
        "question": "How long do VA claims take?",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "VA publishes live timeliness data on VA.gov. Recent averages have been roughly 4–5 months for an initial decision; Board of Veterans' Appeals decisions can take 12–24 months or longer. Check the current average on VA.gov before relying on a specific number."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "faq_item",
        "question": "Can my rating be reduced?",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "Before the 20-year protection mark, yes — the VA can schedule re-examinations if it has reason to believe the condition has improved. Ratings held continuously for 20+ years and ratings designated Permanent and Total receive additional protection."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "faq_item",
        "question": "What's the difference between VA disability and SSDI?",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "VA disability is service-connected compensation paid by the VA with no income limit. SSDI is paid by the Social Security Administration for a disability that prevents substantial work, regardless of cause, and has SSA-defined earnings limits. The two programs are independent and many veterans receive both."
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "type": "faq_item",
        "question": "Do I need a lawyer to file a claim?",
        "spans": [
            {
                "text": "For an initial claim, no. An accredited Veterans Service Organization (VSO) can file the claim at no cost. For appeals after a denial, an accredited attorney or claims agent may charge a fee under VA rules (38 CFR §14.636). Verify accreditation in the VA Office of General Counsel database."
            }
        ]
    }
